Define three more float-to-int conversions UBSan flags in xsf.

The Bessel xsref inputs reach three conversions of out-of-range doubles to
int (undefined behaviour; clang's UBSan and CRAN's UBSan check report them):

- bessel.h, cyl_bessel_j: `v != static_cast<int>(v)` for |v| >= 2^31 now
  counts v as not a whole number, as x86's INT_MIN result did.
- cephes/yv.h, yv: `n = v; if (n == v)` likewise takes the non-integer
  path for |v| >= 2^31, as on x86.
- amos/amos.h, the `inu = (int)fnu` that unk2 reaches (line 5897 upstream):
  inu is used there only for the fractional part and inu % 4, so for
  fnu >= 2^31 both are computed from floor(fnu).

Unchanged below 2^31. Reached by jv(3.16e50, x), yv(5.62e25, x) and the
AMOS path at order 3.16e10. amos.h has nine more `inu = (int)fnu` sites,
each using inu differently; no test reaches them, so they are left as they
are rather than changed blind.

Upstream: https://github.com/scipy/xsf/issues/338

diff -ru a/include/xsf/amos/amos.h b/include/xsf/amos/amos.h
--- a/include/xsf/amos/amos.h	2026-10-24 11:13:46
+++ b/include/xsf/amos/amos.h	2026-10-24 11:13:46
@@ -5894,8 +5894,15 @@
         yy = std::imag(zr);
         zn = -zr * std::complex<double>(0, 1);
         zb = zr;
-        inu = (int)fnu;
-        fnf = fnu - inu;
+        // Patched (zuspecial 0002): (int)fnu is undefined for fnu >= 2^31.
+        // inu is used only for the fractional part and inu % 4.
+        if (fnu < 2147483648.0) {
+            inu = (int)fnu;
+            fnf = fnu - inu;
+        } else {
+            inu = (int)std::fmod(std::floor(fnu), 4.0);
+            fnf = fnu - std::floor(fnu);
+        }
         ang = -hpi * fnf;
         car = std::cos(ang);
         sar = std::sin(ang);
diff -ru a/include/xsf/bessel.h b/include/xsf/bessel.h
--- a/include/xsf/bessel.h	2026-10-24 11:13:46
+++ b/include/xsf/bessel.h	2026-10-24 11:13:46
@@ -909,7 +909,7 @@
 
 template <typename T>
 T cyl_bessel_j(T v, T x) {
-    if (v != static_cast<int>(v) && x < 0) {
+    if ((std::fabs(v) >= 2147483648.0 || v != static_cast<int>(v)) && x < 0) {
         set_error("jv", SF_ERROR_DOMAIN, NULL);
         return std::numeric_limits<T>::quiet_NaN();
     }
